WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:04.816
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.816 --> 00:00:07.120
I am a classic millennial.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.821
I'm highly jaded and
highly optimistic at the same time.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.987
I'm jaded because let's face it,
we're the generation that inherited

00:00:18.987 --> 00:00:22.399
all the consequences of bad
decisions from previous generations.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.000
Climate change, wealth inequality,
broken healthcare.

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.687
And to me, businesses seem to be
the biggest culprits of these problems.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:43.750
But I'm also optimistic that
businesses can be a force for good.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.540
And given the size of the private sector,
businesses need to be forces for

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.730
good if we want real scale change.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.929
So how do we make businesses do good?

00:00:55.659 --> 00:00:58.895
Of course as a millennial I said,
I'm a problem solver.

00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:00.535
I'm gifted and special.

00:01:00.535 --> 00:01:03.015
I'm going to single handedly
solve this problem.

00:01:03.015 --> 00:01:03.870
You're welcome guys.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.115
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; After college,

00:01:07.115 --> 00:01:13.015
I went into investing to understand and
influence how businesses make decisions.

00:01:13.015 --> 00:01:18.922
In my job, I came across a lot of
businesses and a lot of bad decisions.

00:01:18.922 --> 00:01:22.220
Here's one well known example,
the BP oil spill.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.270
In 2010, BP was facing a delay
in their drilling operations.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.621
Every day of delay cost them $1 million.

00:01:30.621 --> 00:01:31.985
Under immense pressure,

00:01:31.985 --> 00:01:34.840
they rushed a launch of an oil
rig in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.130
The rig exploded.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:42.498
Causing the largest oil spill in history,
killing 11 people and

00:01:42.498 --> 00:01:46.562
damaging our planet in ways we
still don't fully know today.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.269
This was costly for society,
but also costly for BP.

00:01:55.269 --> 00:02:01.937
To save $1 million a day,
BP cost it shareholders $100 billion.

00:02:03.642 --> 00:02:06.279
I call this nearsightedness.

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:10.530
Focusing so much on what's in front of you
that you're missing the bigger picture.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.000
Let me ask you a question.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.360
Who here is nearsighted?

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.690
So you have trouble seeing what's far from
you but you can see clearly what's close.

00:02:20.554 --> 00:02:26.480
I am seriously nearsighted and we all
know how dangerous and frustrating it is.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.020
Imagine one day you're outside
without your glasses or

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.910
contacts, you're a disaster
waiting to happen.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.450
You might be scanning the ground for
something you might trip on, and suddenly,

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.760
a pole hits your face.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.940
Traditional business thinking
is near sighted, focusing so

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.570
much on what's in front of you that
you're missing the bigger picture.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:52.310
With this realization, I searched for
a better way to invest in and

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.130
support businesses.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.482
That is putting on a pair of glasses
that would help me see more clearly and

00:02:58.482 --> 00:03:00.050
more comprehensively.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.565
Investing with an impact lens.

00:03:04.798 --> 00:03:10.039
By impact lens I mean, asking yourself
more questions before you make decisions.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:15.320
Is this truly the best decision for the
long-term sustainability of the company?

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:22.000
Can I do this in a way that does good or
reduce harm for society?

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.406
Just imagine if someone at BP
had asked those questions.

00:03:27.494 --> 00:03:33.320
The impact lens is for everyone, not just
impact investors or social entrepreneurs.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.580
It doesn't mean making
bad decisions to do good.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.980
It means making better decisions
to do good and do well.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.900
Your ears better be perking up because
I'm telling you the impact lens can make

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:47.945
you more money.

00:03:47.945 --> 00:03:53.272
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; With every business and

00:03:53.272 --> 00:03:56.083
investment decision you have
to evaluate two things.

00:03:56.083 --> 00:03:58.458
Opportunities and risks.

00:03:58.458 --> 00:04:02.400
The impact lens can help you
see both new opportunities and

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.360
new risks you might not be seeing.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.789
I want to tell you two stories
to show you what I mean.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.506
The first story is about opportunities.

00:04:13.598 --> 00:04:17.917
John Treharne was a former English squash
player and an entrepreneur looking for

00:04:17.917 --> 00:04:19.070
new opportunities.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.160
As an athlete, he noticed that old
gyms in the UK were located in wealthy

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.759
metropolitan areas charging high prices.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:29.310
This made him think.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.230
A low cost gym concept targeting lower
income markets could do a lot of good and

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:35.450
be really successful.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:40.460
So he wrote a business plan and
then he sought out funding.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.080
But investor after investor rejected him.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.030
It wouldn't work, they said.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.970
If lower income markets were so
attractive, gyms would already be there.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:53.390
Then one day he found a match.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:59.000
Bridges, an impact investment fund had
also concluded that low cost gyms would

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.412
be an attractive opportunity to deliver
financial returns and societal impact.

00:05:03.412 --> 00:05:06.044
Together, Treharne and

00:05:06.044 --> 00:05:11.935
Bridges incubated The Gym Group,
a chain of 24/7,

00:05:11.935 --> 00:05:17.088
low cost gyms targeted
at lower income markets.

00:05:17.088 --> 00:05:20.540
The Gym Group was a massive success.

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:26.300
In 2015, the company IPOed at
a valuation of $380 million,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.750
almost 6 times the original investment.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.450
Private equity funds typically
target two to three times.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.939
Today, the Gym Group has more than
a 130 gyms and 660,000 members.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.940
Now, lot of gym space was already crowded.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.810
No one had thought lower income markets
were an attractive opportunity.

00:05:49.875 --> 00:05:55.160
Treharne and Bridges saw this opportunity
because they had their impact lenses on.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.992
So whether you want to start,
run or invest in businesses,

00:05:58.992 --> 00:06:03.915
how can you use the impact lens to see
opportunities you might not be seeing?

00:06:06.964 --> 00:06:10.080
The impact lens also helps
you see hidden risks.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.790
Paul Polman became the CEO
of Unilever in 2009.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:20.420
Polman was a strong believer
in doing well by doing good.

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:24.839
With his impact lens on, he saw
opportunities to grow in emerging markets.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:31.405
He also saw a risk that no one was
talking about, climate change.

00:06:31.405 --> 00:06:35.620
Polman thought climate change was
the single biggest threat to Unilever and

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.455
no investor had ever asked
him a question about it.

00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:42.674
Polman declared, in ten years,
I'm going to double

00:06:42.674 --> 00:06:47.851
profitability while cutting
environmental footprint in half.

00:06:47.851 --> 00:06:52.272
Becoming more environmentally
sustainable was going to cut costs, and

00:06:52.272 --> 00:06:53.810
reduce risks, he said.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.664
People thought he was crazy.

00:06:57.891 --> 00:07:01.590
The results,
eight years of positive growth.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:04.280
Twice the rate of Unilever's competitors.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:10.230
Total shareholder return of
nearly 300% in that time period.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:16.710
Returns in profits are up, but even more
importantly, long term risks are down.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:21.098
Unilever is better prepared for climate
change than any of its competitors.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:27.402
Now climate change is just one
example of a societal issue,

00:07:27.402 --> 00:07:30.440
threatening corporate welfare.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:34.915
Global poverty, employee skills gaps,
political and social instability.

00:07:34.915 --> 00:07:39.730
Nearsighted businesses will say,
these are not my problems.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.194
But if we put our in impact lenses on,

00:07:43.194 --> 00:07:47.396
we can see that these issues will
hit businesses before they know it.

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:54.965
Now I'm not saying that every
decision is going to be a win-win.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:58.969
But win-win situations are out there and

00:07:58.969 --> 00:08:02.470
we absolutely have to chase
every single one of them.

00:08:04.236 --> 00:08:08.890
John Treharne and Paul Polman
are two pioneers in this movement.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.210
Slowly, others are catching up.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:19.100
Just last year, the CEO of BlackRock,
Larry Fink said in a statement to CEOs.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:24.530
To prosper overtime, every business must
not only deliver financial performance but

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.640
also show how it makes a positive
contribution to society.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.368
What we need are more business leaders and

00:08:32.368 --> 00:08:35.694
institutional investors with
their impact lenses on.

00:08:37.413 --> 00:08:41.633
We also need personal investors and
consumers demanding for

00:08:41.633 --> 00:08:44.230
more impact from businesses.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.995
We millennials have to take that charge.

00:08:48.624 --> 00:08:53.730
We're known as the generation of FOMO,
fear of missing out.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.080
Well guess what?

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.521
We're missing out big time because we're
not using the impact lens in our business

00:09:00.521 --> 00:09:01.163
thinking.

00:09:03.486 --> 00:09:06.540
We're also known as the generation
of self entitlement.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:10.296
Well, I think we are entitled.

00:09:11.852 --> 00:09:15.930
To a better future where businesses
can be part of the solution and

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.350
not just the problem.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:24.342
Where a fresh set of lenses can help
us see the world in a better light.

00:09:24.342 --> 00:09:25.037
Thank you.

00:09:25.037 --> 00:09:27.338
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:27.338 --> 00:09:32.154
[MUSIC]

